.Dd July 13, 2026
.Dt __OS_CPU 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm __os_ncpus ,
.Nm __os_ncpus_perf ,
.Nm __os_ncpus_effic ,
.Nm __os_numa_nnodes ,
.Nm __os_numa_node_of_cpu ,
.Nm __os_numa_current_node ,
.Nm __os_mem_max
.Nd CPU/NUMA topology and container-aware resource probes
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In os_cpu.h
.Ft int
.Fn __os_ncpus "void"
.Ft int
.Fn __os_ncpus_perf "void"
.Ft int
.Fn __os_ncpus_effic "void"
.Ft int
.Fn __os_numa_nnodes "void"
.Ft int
.Fn __os_numa_node_of_cpu "int cpu"
.Ft int
.Fn __os_numa_current_node "void"
.Ft int64_t
.Fn __os_mem_max "void"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn __os_ncpus
returns the number of logical CPUs usable by the process.
On Linux it first consults the cgroup v2
.Pa cpu.max
control file
.Pq Pa /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.max :
when it holds a numeric
.Sq quota
and
.Sq period
(microseconds), the effective count is
.Fn ceil quota/period ,
clamped to at least 1 and at most the real hardware count from
.Fn sysconf _SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN .
This makes the default reactor/thread-pool sizing correct inside a
Kubernetes pod or Docker container with a CPU limit, instead of
sizing for cores the process cannot actually schedule on.
A missing or unreadable
.Pa cpu.max ,
or a
.Sq max
(unlimited) quota, falls back to the uncapped host count on every
platform.
.Pp
.Fn __os_ncpus_perf
and
.Fn __os_ncpus_effic
report the performance- and efficiency-core counts on asymmetric
hardware (Apple Silicon); elsewhere every CPU is a "performance" CPU
and the efficiency count is 0.
.Pp
.Fn __os_numa_nnodes ,
.Fn __os_numa_node_of_cpu ,
and
.Fn __os_numa_current_node
report NUMA topology on Linux (single-node elsewhere).
.Pp
.Fn __os_mem_max
returns the usable memory cap in bytes.
On Linux it consults cgroup v2's
.Pa memory.max
.Pq Pa /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.max :
a numeric value is returned directly; a
.Sq max
(unlimited) value, or a missing/unreadable file, falls back to the
host's total physical memory
.Pf ( Fn sysconf _SC_PHYS_PAGES
times
.Fn sysconf _SC_PAGESIZE ) .
Other platforms always report the host total.
.Pp
Both cgroup file paths may be redirected to a fixture file with the
internal test-only hooks
.Fn __xtc_os_cgroup_cpu_path_override
and
.Fn __xtc_os_cgroup_mem_path_override
(Linux only; declared in
.In os_cpu.h ) ,
so the cgroup-parsing logic is unit-testable without root or a real
cgroup.
Pass NULL to restore the real
.Pa /sys/fs/cgroup
path.
Not part of the stable API.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn __os_ncpus ,
.Fn __os_ncpus_perf ,
and
.Fn __os_ncpus_effic
return a count of at least 1 (0 for
.Fn __os_ncpus_effic
on symmetric hardware).
.Fn __os_numa_nnodes
returns a count of at least 1.
.Fn __os_numa_node_of_cpu
and
.Fn __os_numa_current_node
return a NUMA node index (0 if indeterminate).
.Fn __os_mem_max
returns a positive byte count, or 0 if even the host-total fallback
could not be determined.
.Sh THREAD SAFETY
All functions are safe to call concurrently from any thread.
The test-only path-override hooks are not: they are for single-
threaded unit tests only.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
int ncpu = __os_ncpus();               /* container-aware CPU count */
int64_t mem = __os_mem_max();          /* container-aware memory cap */
printf("usable: %d cpus, %lld bytes\en", ncpu, (long long)mem);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr __os_time 3 ,
.Xr xtc_res 3 ,
.Xr xtc 7
.Sh HISTORY
.Fn __os_ncpus
and the NUMA probes first appeared in xtc 0.0.1 (M1).
Cgroup v2 awareness and
.Fn __os_mem_max
were added under PLAN.md 19.15 (M0).
